# Generated protobuf bindings — built by `make gen`, live in-package next to
# the hand-written codec. Source-only at HEAD; vendored at release tags.
# Python:
python/visio_schema/**/*_pb2.py
python/visio_schema/**/*_pb2.pyi
python/visio_schema/foxglove/
# C++ (nanopb only; no host full-protobuf C++ output):
cpp/generated_nanopb/

# buf installed locally via npm at the repo ROOT — a throwaway `npm install
# @bufbuild/buf` must not get committed. Anchored, so the real package under
# ts/ (which HAS a committed package.json) is unaffected.
#
# A root manifest is not merely unwanted, it BREAKS ts/: one declaring
# `workspaces: ["ts"]` makes npm resolve ts/'s lockfile at the root, and
# `npm ci` there fails with "can only install with an existing
# package-lock.json". Consumers reach this package by path, so there is
# nothing a workspace root would buy.
node_modules/
/package.json
/package-lock.json

# The TypeScript package: build output only. Its package.json, tsconfig,
# sources AND its generated bindings are committed.
#
# The bindings are the one generated tree here that IS committed, because a
# consumer resolves this package from a plain checkout: whatever the sibling
# holds is what it gets. Generating them there would put buf — a ~100 MB
# downloaded Go binary — on the install path of every consumer lane, where
# only tsc is otherwise needed. Being committed, they are also the one
# generated tree that can DRIFT from proto/, so ci.yml regenerates and diffs
# them. `make gen-ts` stays the maintainer verb.
ts/dist/
# ...and the corpus copy prepack stages in (ts/scripts/stage-golden.mjs).
ts/golden/

# C++ build trees + Python build/test artifacts.
build/
dist/
wheelhouse/
cpp/build/
examples/cpp/build/
__pycache__/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Compiled native extension (_creader), built in-place by `pip install -e` /
# build_ext. Source-only at HEAD; the wheel ships the binary.
*.so
*.pyd
*.dylib

# Sibling cpp/nanopb sources vendored under python/ at build time (so setuptools
# accepts them); regenerated by _vendor_native.py from cpp/ + third_party/.
python/_native_build/
